  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Crowded cuplet (Merismodes fasciculata) is a tiny, cup-shaped fungus belonging to the Cyphellopsidaceae family, characterized by its densely clustered fruiting bodies. This wood-decay fungus is commonly found in New England and plays an important ecological role in decomposing dead woody material.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cup-shaped fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Distinctive small, cuplike or funnel-shaped ascomata arranged in crowded clusters",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and color",
      "description": "Typically 1-3mm in diameter with pale cream to whitish coloration",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Substrate preference",
      "description": "Found on decaying hardwood, bark, or woody debris rather than on living plants",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Crowded cuplet thrives on dead wood and decaying bark in moist, shaded forest habitats throughout New England, particularly in areas with high humidity and decomposing logs.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its microscopic size, Merismodes fasciculata is a crucial decomposer that helps recycle nutrients back into forest ecosystems by breaking down tough woody material that few other organisms can digest.",
